\b Processing SQL Statements\par
\b0 Once your input is validated (Database(s), DB User, SQL*Plus Options, SQL- and log-file), SQLAgain loops over the selected Databases, builds the parameter string for SQL*Plus and starts sql.exe with the Process.Start method in  a new window.\par
Because there is no direct interaction between you and SQL*Plus, SQLAgain monitors the output of SQL*Plus and kills the process, when one of the following ORA-Errors is trapped:\line\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li720\lang2055 ORA-01017\tab invalid username/password; logon denied\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}ORA-12170 \tab TNS:Connect timeout occurred\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}\lang1033 ORA-12154 \tab TNS: could not resolve the connect identifier specified\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}ORA-12514\tab TNS listener does not currently know of service requested in connect descriptor\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}ORA-12545 \tab Connect failed because target host or object does not exist \par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}\lang2055 ORA-28001\tab The password has expired\par

\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlcont\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\par
\lang1033 Other error conditions are not monitored, neither is a TIMEOUT for long running statements defined.\par
 \par
Sometimes SQL*Plus seems to hang - maybe it prompts for a new password ?\par
You may kill the SQL*PlusSession by pressing the button \b Cancel SQL\b0\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\nowidctlpar\fi-360\li720 this button is only visible when \i Execute \i0 is in progress.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}it will kill the SQL*Plus Processtree and skip all checked, not yet processed Databases\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\par
\b SQL*Plus Options - managing formatting Options\par
\b0 First Option \b -S Silent\b0  has an impact on the reported output:\par
\par
\b Option -S Silent not checked \b0 (Default):  SQL*Plus Banner and your SQL Statements in the SQL File are displayed\par
\f1\fs16\par
\b\f0\fs18 Option -S Silent checked \b0 results in no banner and no command prompt\b\f1\fs16\par
\f2\par
\f0\fs18 Option HTML, plus Option -S Silent\par
\b0 SQLAgain sets the logfilename to *.html and uses a different startup file (SQLAgainStart_HTML.sql) to SET SQLFORMAT HTML. The Banner and Trailer in the logfile are surrounded by HTML <pre> </pre> tags\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl240\slmult1\f3\fs22\lang7\par

\pard\nowidctlpar\f0\fs18\lang1033\par
\par
\b Option CSV ON plus Option -S Silent\par
\b0 Similar action for option CSV: startup file SQLAgainStart_CSV.sql including SET SQLFORMAT CSV is used, the mime-type for the logfile is changed to .csv\par
\par
SQL*Plus supports more SQLFORMAT options like insert | loader | xml | json. You might specify them in your SQL-File as SET SQLFORMAT or as optimizer-hint option.\par
\par
\par
More details to the markup options: {\cf0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/19/sqpug/generating-HTML-reports-from-SQL-Plus.html }}{\fldrslt{https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/19/sqpug/generating-HTML-reports-from-SQL-Plus.html\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs18\par
\par
\b Timeout for SQL*Plus\par
\b0 Maybe you have a very intensive list of DML statements, very low priority, but you are afraid that the job runs into the production online window ?\par
Ensure, that the sql file contains COMMIT statements after each logical block of statements and define in option \b Timeout for SQL*Plus \b0 the number of seconds a single SQL*Plus session may run. \par
Test it with SQL Statement \b exec dbms_lock.sleep(10);  \b0 and a Timeout < 10 seconds.\par
The default of 0 seconds means: ignore Timeout.\b\par
\par
Status Messages\par
\b0 Are messages from the current session. When processing multiple Databases, the start time, function and Database name is displayed before SQL*Plus starts. This way you should recognize easier which Database blocks.\par
Important Messages are written to file Session History.\par
\b\par
Favorites\b0\par
Here you've got the possibility to save the current selected options (List of selected DBs, DB-User, SQL File, SQL*Plus options) as Favorite.\line Favorites could be executed by a mouse double click or with context-menu option \b Execute\b0 .\line Other context-menu options: \b Load\b0 , \b Edit Description,\b0  \b Delete \b0 o\b r Update\b0 .\par
Want to update a favorite ? \b Load\b0 , change your selections, \b Delete \b0 and \b Add Favorite \b0 does the trick.\par
\par
\b Session History\b0\par
Are trace records from SQLAgain saved in C:\\ProgramData\\SQLAgain\\Sessions.log. \par
SQLAgain reads the file at startup and deletes records older than 6 month automatically. \par

\pard\nowidctlpar\sl240\slmult1\f3\fs22\lang7\par
